    rdfs:label "leaching: 97.1 % for Li under HCl 3 M; S/L 1:50; 90 min; 80 °C; without H2O2" ;
    weee:conditionLabel "HCl 3 M; S/L 1:50; 90 min; 80 °C; without H2O2" ;
    weee:description "hydrochloric acid leaching of calcined anode powder without H2O2 has a leaching observation for Li as 97.1 %. Source study: Leaching Lithium from the Anode Electrode Materials of Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries by Hydrochloric Acid (Hcl). Conditions: HCl 3 M; S/L 1:50; 90 min; 80 °C; without H2O2." ;
    weee:evidenceSpan "the leaching efficiency of Li was 97.1% without H2O2" ;
    weee:observationProperty "leaching" ;
    weee:observationUnit "%" ;
    weee:observationValue 97.1 ;
    weee:originalRelationType "HAS_OUTPUT" ;
    weee:settingCondition "HCl 3 M; S/L 1:50; 90 min; 80 °C; without H2O2" ;
    weee:sourceEntityEvidence "Fig. 9 shows the alteration of the leaching efficiency of Li as a function of HCl - H2O2 ratio in the range of 1:0–25:1 by using HCl concentration of 3 M, S/L of 1:50, leaching time of 90 min and leaching temperature of 80 ^∘C. It can be observed from Fig. 7 that the leaching efficiency of Li was 97.1% without H2O2. And it reached to 99.4% with HCl - H2O2 ration being 10:1. A conclusion can be drawn from the experiment that H2O2 had a slight role in the leaching process of Li. In the leaching process for Hydrometallurgy, H2O2 was added as reductant to convert undissolved high valence ions to soluble low ones to promote the reaction process. Because of the absence of intermediate valence of Li, H2O2 can reduce Co(III) to Co (II) and thus facilitate the forward reaction. The result shows that the amount of H2O2 was sufficient for the complete dissolution of lithium, as further increasing the concentration of H2O2 did not show a significant effect in the recovery efficiency compared to consumption of expensive of lithium. Considering lower energy consumption and relatively better leaching efficiency, the suitable leaching agent was HCl without H2O2." ;
    weee:sourcePaper "Leaching Lithium from the Anode Electrode Materials of Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries by Hydrochloric Acid (Hcl)" ;
    weee:sourceRelationId "REL_reviewed_283200192ce04a28" ;
    weee:sourceSample "calcined powder" ;
    rel:observedMaterial <http://weee-kg.org/entity/Li> .

<http://weee-kg.org/source:Leaching%20Lithium%20from%20the%20Anode%20Electrode%20Materials%20of%20Spent%20Lithium-Ion%20Batteries%20by%20Hydrochloric%20Acid%20%28Hcl%29:Observation_REL_reviewed_a9a09b1adf9bea5e_766f1df6e3b81975> a weee:Observation ;
    rdfs:label "leaching: 84 % for Li under deionized water only; without HCl" ;
    weee:conditionLabel "deionized water only; without HCl" ;
    weee:description "deionized water leaching of calcined anode powder has a leaching observation for Li as 84.0 %. Source study: Leaching Lithium from the Anode Electrode Materials of Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries by Hydrochloric Acid (Hcl). Conditions: deionized water only; without HCl." ;
    weee:evidenceSpan "in the experiment performed it has a high leaching efficiency of lithium (84%) only in deionized water" ;
    weee:observationProperty "leaching" ;
    weee:observationUnit "%" ;
    weee:observationValue 84.0 ;
    weee:originalRelationType "HAS_OUTPUT" ;
    weee:settingCondition "deionized water only; without HCl" ;
    weee:sourceEntityEvidence "As described above, the main forms of Li in the anode active material are Li2CO3,, Li O, LiF, ROCO Li, CH OLi. Because some of them are water-soluble, such as ROCO2 Li CH3OLi Li2O,, in the experiment performed it has a high leaching efficiency of lithium (84%) only in deionized water. While others are almost insoluble in water and inlaid in the anode active materials, such as ROCO Li, LiF, and they will occur decomposition reaction in HCl solution. Therefore, the leaching reaction between anode materials and HCl solution is a multiple process (Aurbach et al., 2002; Jiangfeng et al., 2004): (1) the hydrolysis of lithium salt (2) double decomposition reaction between lithium salt and HCl solution." ;
    weee:sourcePaper "Leaching Lithium from the Anode Electrode Materials of Spent Lithium-Ion Batteries by Hydrochloric Acid (Hcl)" ;
    weee:sourceRelationId "REL_reviewed_a9a09b1adf9bea5e" ;
    rel:observedMaterial <http://weee-kg.org/entity/Li> .

